1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,760
The guy next to you in Economics is number 412.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:09,930
Not 412 in the class. 412 on Earth. Out of eight billion people.

3
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:10,320
And you?

4
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:13,300
You're number 6,914,000,000.

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:15,020
He's drooling on his notes.

6
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:17,120
You've done every reading this semester.

7
00:00:17,680 --> 00:00:19,520
Something is seriously wrong here.

8
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:23,500
By the end of this video you'll find out what the number really measures, who is

9
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:28,080
ranked number 3 on the entire planet, and how one sentence moved you billions of

10
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:29,560
places in five seconds.

11
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:32,320
But first, this morning, when it started.

12
00:00:32,860 --> 00:00:36,440
You wake up, you brush your teeth, and the first thing you notice is a number

13
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:38,320
floating above your own head in the mirror.

14
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:39,680
You try to wave it away.

15
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:42,320
It follows your hand like a balloon on a string.

16
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:45,700
Your roommate Dev walks in eating cereal straight from the box.

17
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:47,580
Two billion, 200 million.

18
00:00:48,140 --> 00:00:49,460
Why are you staring at my forehead?

19
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:50,220
No reason.

20
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:52,640
Out in the hallway, everyone has one.

21
00:00:52,980 --> 00:00:55,220
The RA: 788 million.

22
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:57,380
The girl who plays guitar at 2 in the morning:

23
00:00:57,380 --> 00:00:58,920
3.3 billion.

24
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:01,500
The campus dog has no number at all.

25
00:01:01,940 --> 00:01:02,320
Lucky dog.

26
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:04,600
The vending machine that ate your dollar on Monday?

27
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:06,230
Eight billion and one.

28
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:07,000
That one makes sense.

29
00:01:07,420 --> 00:01:08,820
By lunch you've worked out the rule.

30
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:10,880
One is the best human alive.

31
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:12,930
Eight billion is the worst.

32
00:01:12,980 --> 00:01:13,900
Everyone gets ranked.

33
00:01:14,280 --> 00:01:15,360
Nobody else can see it.

34
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:18,590
And nobody explains what the ranking actually measures.

35
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:19,920
Your first guess is money.

36
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:21,160
So you test it.

37
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:25,040
The richest kid on campus, the one who parks his car across two spaces, is

38
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:26,440
number 1.9 billion.

39
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:27,360
Not great.

40
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:31,460
The woman mopping the dining hall floor is 9,844.

41
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:34,500
She is ranked higher than every student in this building.

42
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:35,960
So it's not money.

43
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:37,640
You try celebrities on your phone.

44
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:38,360
Nothing.

45
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:40,320
Numbers only show up in person.

46
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:41,500
Dev sits down.

47
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:42,880
You tell him everything.

48
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:46,680
So you're saying you can see a number over my head, and it's two billion.

49
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:47,860
2.2.

50
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:48,720
Is that good?

51
00:01:49,140 --> 00:01:49,920
It's right in the middle.

52
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:50,460
Mid.

53
00:01:50,840 --> 00:01:50,980
Cool.

54
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:51,800
Cool cool cool.

55
00:01:52,180 --> 00:01:52,820
He eats a fry.

56
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:53,520
What's yours?

57
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:54,640
You don't answer.

58
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,160
So you keep testing.

59
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:01,060
Your Economics professor, who has three degrees and wrote the textbook: 1.45

60
00:02:01,060 --> 00:02:01,420
billion.

61
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:02,760
Not looks either.

62
00:02:03,180 --> 00:02:05,260
Half the athletes in the gym are in the billions,

63
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:08,900
and a guy with spaghetti sauce on his shirt is number 80,000.

64
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:13,500
The barista who always remembers your name: 640,000.

65
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:16,280
He's never once spelled it right, but he remembers it.

66
00:02:16,600 --> 00:02:20,440
And the guy who plays loud music on the bus every morning without headphones?

67
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:22,060
7.99 billion.

68
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:24,320
You feel very good about that one.

69
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:26,420
Okay, Dev says, do me.

70
00:02:26,760 --> 00:02:27,380
What's my thing?

71
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:28,980
Why am I 2.2?

72
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:30,100
I don't know yet.

73
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:31,080
That's the problem.

74
00:02:31,540 --> 00:02:34,140
I can see the score, but nobody told me the game.

75
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:35,140
Did it go up?

76
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:35,780
No.

77
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:37,180
I was sitting up straight.

78
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:38,720
It's not posture, Dev.

79
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:43,440
And then, in the library, you see the one number you really didn't want to see.

80
00:02:43,880 --> 00:02:44,640
Then you see Maya.

81
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:48,140
Third row of the library, same seat every Thursday.

82
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:50,160
31,207.

83
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:54,860
Out of eight billion people, she's in the top 32,000.

84
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:56,980
You are in the bottom billion.

85
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:00,400
If the ranking means anything, she is way out of your league.

86
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:02,260
Bro. Go talk to her.

87
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:03,900
She's 31,000.

88
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:07,200
I don't know what that means, but it sounds like a lot of thousands.

89
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:07,860
Go.

90
00:03:08,320 --> 00:03:09,020
You walk over.

91
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,740
On the way, a freshman drops her pen, and you hand it back without thinking.

92
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:14,440
And then you notice something strange.

93
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:16,000
Your number twitches.

94
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:17,280
Just a few digits.

95
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:18,920
It drops by 22.

96
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:21,480
Hi. You're in my Econ section, right?

97
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:24,560
You say the smoothest thing you've ever said in your life.

98
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:27,180
Yes. Section. Econ. Me.

99
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:29,320
That twitch changes everything.

100
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:32,060
If the number can move, you can move it.

101
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:32,820
So you try.

102
00:03:33,180 --> 00:03:33,920
You go to the gym.

103
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:34,600
Nothing.

104
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:36,120
You get an A on your essay.

105
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:36,840
Nothing.

106
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:39,000
You spend half your savings on sneakers.

107
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:39,880
Nothing.

108
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:41,280
Dev claps anyway.

109
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:45,280
Then, on the way out of the dining hall, you hold the door for a crowd of people

110
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:46,120
carrying trays.

111
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:48,720
And the number drops by almost 6,000.

112
00:03:48,720 --> 00:03:50,080
You hold the door again.

113
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:50,800
Nothing.

114
00:03:51,240 --> 00:03:52,000
The crowd is gone.

115
00:03:52,340 --> 00:03:54,540
It only counted when it actually helped someone.

116
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:55,880
You run back to Dev.

117
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:56,760
It moved.

118
00:03:57,060 --> 00:03:58,200
It actually moved.

119
00:03:58,600 --> 00:03:59,380
What did you do?

120
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:00,540
I held a door.

121
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:01,440
For who?

122
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:03,460
Like, 12 people with trays.

123
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:07,860
Dev thinks about this for exactly one second, then sprints to the dining hall

124
00:04:07,860 --> 00:04:12,140
and holds the door open for nobody, for 10 minutes, while people walk around him

125
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:12,920
through the other door.

126
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:14,240
His number doesn't move.

127
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:15,300
Not even a little.

128
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:17,020
It's broken, he says.

129
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:18,140
It's not broken.

130
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:20,180
You just don't understand it yet.

131
00:04:20,820 --> 00:04:22,760
That night, you finally figure it out.

132
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:24,760
And it's so much worse than you thought.

133
00:04:25,380 --> 00:04:26,660
That night you fill a notebook.

134
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:27,640
The cleaner.

135
00:04:28,100 --> 00:04:28,520
The door.

136
00:04:28,980 --> 00:04:30,500
The richest kid who never tips.

137
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:32,780
The number isn't measuring what you have.

138
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:34,960
It's measuring what you do for other people.

139
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:36,420
You remember the cleaner.

140
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:39,120
You saw her hand a lost wallet back to a freshman.

141
00:04:39,620 --> 00:04:42,300
She's done that kind of thing every day for 30 years.

142
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:43,760
And number 412,

143
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:45,340
asleep in Economics?

144
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:49,520
You find out later he works nights as a hospital orderly to pay his tuition.

145
00:04:50,020 --> 00:04:50,680
He's not lazy.

146
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:53,040
He's exhausted from looking after strangers.

147
00:04:53,580 --> 00:04:56,700
And Maya isn't sitting in the library every Thursday because she likes the

148
00:04:56,700 --> 00:04:57,040
chairs.

149
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:59,060
She runs free tutoring for freshmen.

150
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:01,240
That's why she's 31,000.

151
00:05:01,460 --> 00:05:03,100
Which raises a much worse question.

152
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:07,020
If the number measures how much good you do, what have you been doing for 19

153
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:09,280
years to end up at 6.9 billion?

154
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:10,780
You start remembering.

155
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:14,640
The time you walked past someone carrying a box twice their size because you

156
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:16,220
were late, even though you weren't.

157
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:18,320
The calls from your mom you let go to voicemail.

158
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:21,240
The notes you borrowed freshman year and never paid back.

159
00:05:21,700 --> 00:05:22,580
None of it was evil.

160
00:05:22,940 --> 00:05:23,520
That's the thing.

161
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:25,340
You never did anything terrible.

162
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:27,540
You just never did anything at all.

163
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:31,800
And then, on the bus home, you see the lowest number you've ever seen.

164
00:05:32,380 --> 00:05:32,620
Three.

165
00:05:33,200 --> 00:05:33,940
The bus driver.

166
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:37,680
He says good morning to every single person who gets on, by name.

167
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:39,880
He waits for the student running late in the rain.

168
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:43,480
He keeps a box of spare umbrellas under his seat, and you've watched him give

169
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:45,000
away five of them this month.

170
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:45,920
Rough day, kid?

171
00:05:46,380 --> 00:05:47,220
How do you...

172
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:48,420
why do you do all this?

173
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:49,460
He shrugs.

174
00:05:49,740 --> 00:05:51,040
Somebody's gotta make the bus nice.

175
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:52,780
Might as well be the guy driving it.

176
00:05:53,060 --> 00:05:54,920
Number three on the entire planet.

177
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:56,560
And he's never going to know.

178
00:05:56,980 --> 00:05:58,220
So now you know the rule.

179
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:01,360
And knowing the rule turns out to be the most dangerous part.

180
00:06:01,760 --> 00:06:03,180
Here's the problem with knowing the rule.

181
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:04,540
You start gaming it.

182
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:06,880
You pick up litter, but only where people can see.

183
00:06:07,180 --> 00:06:09,840
You volunteer for one hour and post about it for three.

184
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:13,720
You help an old man cross the street, and he did not want to cross the street.

185
00:06:14,180 --> 00:06:15,180
The number doesn't move.

186
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:16,100
Not once.

187
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:18,340
It turns out it can tell the difference.

188
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:20,240
You've been weird all week.

189
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:21,460
You haven't slept,

190
00:06:21,860 --> 00:06:23,160
you keep looking at my head,

191
00:06:23,520 --> 00:06:25,540
and you tried to tutor the campus dog.

192
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:27,600
I'm trying to be a better person.

193
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:29,960
By yelling you're welcome at strangers?

194
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:32,380
Then the group project happens.

195
00:06:33,040 --> 00:06:33,960
Dev did half of it.

196
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:35,740
Your name is on the title slide.

197
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:38,040
His is in tiny letters at the bottom.

198
00:06:38,420 --> 00:06:41,920
The professor is about to hand out the grade, and he asks who did the research.

199
00:06:42,380 --> 00:06:42,900
You could take

200
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:45,180
the credit. Nobody would ever know.

201
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:46,380
Except the number would.

202
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:47,700
Dev did the research.

203
00:06:48,160 --> 00:06:48,580
All of it.

204
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:50,300
I just made the slides look nice.

205
00:06:50,740 --> 00:06:52,000
Your number starts falling.

206
00:06:52,340 --> 00:06:53,580
And not by a few digits.

207
00:06:54,060 --> 00:06:54,700
By billions.

208
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:57,220
Because it was never about the one moment.

209
00:06:57,600 --> 00:07:00,920
It's every time you could have taken something and gave it away instead.

210
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:05,740
Every one you'd skipped for 19 years was still there, waiting to be counted.

211
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:07,940
It stops at 1.2 billion.

212
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:09,280
Still not great.

213
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:12,680
But for the first time, you know exactly how to change it.

214
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:14,060
That was a nice thing you did.

215
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:15,980
Most people would've just taken the grade.

216
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:17,240
It was his work.

217
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:18,960
Want to help with tutoring on Thursday?

218
00:07:19,400 --> 00:07:20,700
We need someone who's good at Econ.

219
00:07:21,080 --> 00:07:22,380
And at, um, section,

220
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:23,140
Econ,

221
00:07:23,460 --> 00:07:23,600
me.

222
00:07:24,140 --> 00:07:25,680
So on Thursday you show up.

223
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:29,900
A freshman named Priya is about to fail Economics and is too embarrassed to ask

224
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:30,260
anyone.

225
00:07:30,620 --> 00:07:33,120
You explain supply and demand using vending machines.

226
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:35,280
Specifically, the one that eats dollars.

227
00:07:35,840 --> 00:07:36,500
She gets it.

228
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:40,320
And the look on her face when she gets it is better than anything you saw your

229
00:07:40,320 --> 00:07:41,420
number do all week.

230
00:07:41,880 --> 00:07:42,780
You don't check your number.

231
00:07:43,200 --> 00:07:45,220
For the first time, you forget it's there.

232
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:47,300
Dev shows up with snacks for everyone.

233
00:07:47,740 --> 00:07:48,560
Nobody asked him to.

234
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:49,800
His number twitches.

235
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:50,880
Did it move?

236
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:51,620
he whispers.

237
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:52,520
Yeah.

238
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:53,660
Was it the snacks?

239
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:54,980
It was the snacks.

240
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:56,980
He has never been happier.

241
00:07:57,380 --> 00:07:59,420
A week later you realize you've stopped checking.

242
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:00,760
The number's still there.

243
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:02,360
You just don't need it anymore.

244
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:04,860
Dev is 1.9 billion now.

245
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:06,060
He's very proud.

246
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:07,660
He made you write it on the fridge.

247
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:10,970
And the vending machine is still eight billion and one.

248
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:12,080
Some things never change.

249
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:13,540
So here's my question.

250
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:16,640
If you could see everyone's rank, would you want to see yours?

251
00:08:17,160 --> 00:08:18,440
Or would you rather never know?

252
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:19,520
Tell me in the comments.

